    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d692">

                <pb facs="0517" n="513" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg513"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.116 Standard Vacuum Oil Co./63:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hankow</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Josselyn</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-10-26T11:00:00+08:00">October 26, 1938—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 27—4 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>17. Standard Vacuum reports its vessel <hi rendition="#italic">Mei Heng</hi>
                    machine gunned October 19 in river at Changsha by Japanese airplane which dived
                    over ship. Vessel hit by fire but no injuries or deaths. Planes later <pb
                        facs="0518" n="514" xml:id="pg514"/> machine gunned immediate vicinity
                    Socony pontoons. Vessels plainly identified as American. In Shanghai’s [1344]
                    October 17, 6 p.m.,<anchor n="26a" xml:id="d692-f1"/> Japanese naval memorandum,
                    paragraph 4, compliments Standard Oil Company on steps taken to render its ships
                    bearing on the question recognizable.</p>
                <p>Shanghai repeat to Tokyo. Repeated to Peiping, Chungking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Josselyn</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
